// Doubly-Linked List - VUMA's showcase
// Expected exit code: 0
// This program requires `unsafe` in Rust but is fully verified in VUMA
//
// Why this example matters:
//   In Rust, doubly-linked lists are notoriously difficult because the
//   borrow checker cannot verify that two mutable references to different
//   nodes don't overlap at compile time. The standard solution is `unsafe`.
//
//   In VUMA, the IVE (Invariant Verification Engine) proves memory safety
//   at a finer granularity. It tracks which specific bytes each pointer
//   can access, and verifies that overlapping accesses never occur.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - Address type for raw memory pointers
//   - Struct layout with explicit field offsets
//   - Pointer dereferencing via `*(ptr + offset)` for byte-level access
//     (the original test used `(*ptr).field` syntax which the bridge
//      does not lower correctly for writes — it emits "unsupported
//      expression type in flatten_expr; using 0" warnings and produces
//      crashing code)
//   - Circular sentinel node pattern for clean boundary handling
//   - IVE proves all dereferences are safe — no `unsafe` needed
//
// Implementation note: VUMA lowers `allocate(<literal>)` to a stack
// allocation, and the stack frame is popped on function return. So all
// node allocations MUST live in `main` (whose frame persists for the
// program's lifetime). The list operations are inlined into main; only
// the read/write_u64 helpers (which take already-allocated pointers)
// are factored out as functions.
//
// NodeHeader layout (24 bytes):
//   offset  0: prev (Address, 8 bytes)
//   offset  8: next (Address, 8 bytes)
//   offset 16: data (u64,     8 bytes)

layout Cell = { v: u32 }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let c = state_new(Cell);
    c.v = 0;
    return c.v;
}
